Yes — this is an important distinction. **`boot/` and `rootfs/` are not C++ project source directories.** They are deployment artifacts/filesystem areas that become part of the **BeagleBone Black SD-card image**.

Also, the structure should be:

```text
deployment/sd-card/
├── boot/
└── rootfs/
```

**not**

```text
boot/
└── rootfs/
```

They are two separate areas.

## 1. Where do `boot/` and `rootfs/` come from?

Your C++ project produces the application:

```text
app/src/*.cpp
       |
       v
   cross compiler
       |
       v
boxes              <-- ARM executable
```

But the BeagleBone cannot boot from `boxes` alone.

It needs:

```text
Bootloader
Linux Kernel
Device Tree
Root Filesystem
Application
```

So the deployment process combines everything:

```text
C++ Source
    |
    v
boxes executable
    |
    +--------------------+
                         |
Yocto / BSP              |
    |                    |
    +--> U-Boot          |
    +--> Linux Kernel    |
    +--> Device Tree     |
    +--> RootFS          |
                         |
                         v
                  SD Card Image
                         |
              +----------+----------+
              |                     |
              v                     v
           BOOT                  ROOTFS
```

---

# 2. What is `boot/`?

`boot/` represents the **boot partition** of the SD card.

For BeagleBone Black, conceptually:

```text
SD Card
│
├── BOOT partition
│   │
│   ├── MLO
│   ├── u-boot.img
│   ├── Image
│   └── am335x-boneblack.dtb
│
└── ROOTFS partition
    │
    ├── bin/
    ├── etc/
    ├── lib/
    ├── usr/
    ├── var/
    └── opt/
```

### Boot files

| File                   | Purpose                      |
| ---------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| `MLO`                  | SPL / first-stage bootloader |
| `u-boot.img`           | U-Boot bootloader            |
| `Image`                | Linux kernel                 |
| `am335x-boneblack.dtb` | Hardware description         |

The exact filenames can vary depending on your BSP/build system.

---

# 3. What is `rootfs/`?

`rootfs/` means **Root Filesystem**.

This is the complete Linux filesystem that Linux mounts after the kernel starts.

For example:

```text
rootfs/
├── bin/
├── boot/
├── dev/
├── etc/
├── home/
├── lib/
├── media/
├── mnt/
├── opt/
├── proc/
├── root/
├── run/
├── sbin/
├── sys/
├── tmp/
├── usr/
└── var/
```

Your application gets installed inside this root filesystem.

For example:

```text
rootfs/
└── opt/
    └── box-storage/
        ├── bin/
        │   └── boxes
        │
        └── config/
            ├── boxes.conf
            └── logging.conf
```

And your systemd service:

```text
rootfs/
└── etc/
    └── systemd/
        └── system/
            └── box-storage.service
```

---

# 4. How does your project enter `rootfs`?

This is the important part.

Your source:

```text
app/
├── include/
├── src/
└── tests/
```

gets compiled:

```text
Date.cpp
Entry.cpp
StaticBox.cpp
DynamicBox.cpp
BoxManager.cpp
main.cpp
             |
             v
      ARM cross compiler
             |
             v
        boxes executable
```

Then installation:

```text
boxes
  |
  v
rootfs/opt/box-storage/bin/boxes
```

Configuration:

```text
config/
├── boxes.conf
└── logging.conf
        |
        v
rootfs/opt/box-storage/config/
```

Systemd:

```text
deployment/systemd/
└── box-storage.service
        |
        v
rootfs/etc/systemd/system/
└── box-storage.service
```

So:

```text
                 YOUR PROJECT
                     |
       +-------------+-------------+
       |             |             |
       v             v             v
     Source       Config       systemd
       |             |             |
       v             v             v
    Compile        Copy          Copy
       |             |             |
       +-------------+-------------+
                     |
                     v
                  ROOTFS
                     |
          +----------+----------+
          |          |          |
          v          v          v
        /opt       /etc       /var
       boxes      systemd     lib
```

---

# 5. But where does the rest of rootfs come from?

This is where **Yocto** comes in.

You do **not** manually create:

```text
rootfs/bin
rootfs/lib
rootfs/usr
rootfs/sbin
```

for a real embedded Linux project.

Yocto creates them.

Conceptually:

```text
Yocto
  |
  +-- BusyBox
  +-- glibc
  +-- Linux libraries
  +-- systemd
  +-- kernel modules
  +-- configuration
  +-- your application
  |
  v
Complete RootFS
```

For your project:

```text
Yocto RootFS
      |
      +-- Standard Linux files
      |
      +-- systemd
      |
      +-- libraries
      |
      +-- box-storage application
      |
      +-- configuration
      |
      v
rootfs/
```

---

# 6. Where does `boot/` come from?

Again, normally **Yocto/BSP generates it**.

Conceptually:

```text
Yocto
 |
 +-- U-Boot
 |     |
 |     +-- MLO
 |     +-- u-boot.img
 |
 +-- Linux Kernel
 |     |
 |     +-- Image
 |
 +-- Device Tree
       |
       +-- am335x-boneblack.dtb
```

Then these are assembled into the boot partition.

So you shouldn't think:

> "I need to manually write MLO and U-Boot."

Instead think:

> **"The BeagleBone BSP/Yocto build generates the boot artifacts, and my deployment process packages them into the SD-card image."**

